Mailer 1.24 Release Notes
-------------------------

CHANGE FROM "BITNET" TO "BSMTP" AS THE OUTGOING EXIT OF CHOICE
-------------------------------------------------------------- 
As I hope all of you saw in my recent postings to the MAIL-L and
XMAILER mailing lists, a new version of the MG program (which
generates the MAILER PROFILE from the various NAMES files) will change
the default exit for a mailer from "BITNET" to "BSMTP".  This has the
following impact:

- Pre-version 1.24 MAILERs will generate a lot of "junk" BSMTP REPLY
  files.  Version 1.24 MAILERs will also do the same thing when they
  receive BSMTP requests from pre-version 1.24 MAILERs (that supply
  a VERB command).

- Non-BSMTP Mailers that are registered with the :mailer.vmid tag
  and lacking any specific outgoing exit type will have to change
  their registration with BITNIC.  So far,  only one site has
  such a Mailer and will appear in the next XMAILER names with an
  updated :mailer tag.

Because of this, Mailer 1.24 will be released in two phases.  The
first release will include Mailer 1.24 proper but with the old MG
program.  The second release will follow several weeks later and will
include the new MG program.  You are encouraged to install Mailer 1.24
as soon as possible.


LIST OF FILES
-------------
The following files will be sent with the first phase 1.24 release:

    OFFER   MEMO
    INSTALL LISTING
    MAILER  MODULE
    MAILER  TXTLIB

Please read INSTALL LISTING.  It has been substantially rewritten
and expanded.


BUG FIXES
---------
RSCS-rejected files (i.e. referring to a misspelled or otherwise
nonexistent node name) are now returned to the mail sender and not
the postmaster.  (The S#NXFER SITEOPTS flag's meaning has been reversed
by this.)

A protection exception when attempting to parse a null address was
fixed by Mike Borkowski of Waterloo.

Most valid addresses presented to the BSMTP module now parse properly.
Previsouly, some valid addresses caused a syntax error.  Fix thanks
to Andy Hooper of Queens University.  Full RFC821 address are still
not supported.

MAILSMSI ASSEMBLE will now assemble under VM/SP 4.

Error mail resulting from mail sent via a LOCAL mail server (e.g.
SMTPSERV, MLNET, LISTSERV) will be returned to the mail sender
and no longer to the server.

BSMTP "dot doubling" now works.  It previously caused an extra line
to be generated for each line starting with a dot.

The BSMTP OUTGOING exit no longer turns mail addressed to
"foo"%xyz@bar into RCPT TO:<"foo"@bar>.

Type 2 OUTGOING exits (e.g. BITNET) are now properly "chained"
together by the module that builds the list of exits to call
(BUILDLST).  Previously, two instantiations of the same exit but with
different server node/vmid would result in only one invocation of the
exit.  The criterion for a single invocation of a type 2 exit is now
(as it should have been) a match of:
	- exit name
	- server node
	- server userid

An improved error message tells you that there is a duplicate table
entry in the MAILER PROFILE instead of providing a cryptic "table
add error 08" message.


NEW FEATURES
------------

Messages are now prefixed by "*" (for GONE).  Multiline local messages
sent as command replies have a final marker line that indicates the
end of the message group so that IUCV-trapping programs can determine
when the end of a group of replies has been received.

BSMTP support has been substantially changed/improved:
	- The MAILER version is included in the "220" service ready message.
	- A better "Received" message is generated.  See INSTALL LISTING
	  for details.
	- BSMTP REPLYs (when sent) are sent class M (was B).
	- BSMTP REPLYs are now only sent when a VERB command appeared
	  or when there was an error and the "RPLY SMTP" command appeared.
	  By default, errors are sent as mail to the sender.
	- BSMTP REPLYs when received by MAILER are now parsed and will
	  cause error mail to be sent to the original mail sender if
	  the reply indicates an error code.
	- A failure reply from the end-of-DATA command (".") includes
	  the specific error messages indicating the reason for failure.
	- The :READ command has been added to ignore CMS PUNCH command headers.

The INTERNET exit includes Henry's 1/29/87 version plus Jeff's updates
which include better error messages.

The LOCAL exit has substantially improved PROFS "emulation" thanks
to Peter Day of EMU.  See also the PROFS exit.

The BSMTP exit no longer generates a VERB command.  It also has two
new types of parameter (see INSTALL LISTING) which allow both RFC821
"at-domain-list" routing and the "percent routing hack."

Two new MAILER PROFILE variables have been defined:
	- Allow-Charlatans=TRUE tells it to allow BSMTP mail from
	  unregistered mailers to contain domains and mailboxes that
	  do not strictly match the RSCS file origin.  When this
	  happens, comments in the "Received" header and/or the
	  presence of an "X-Delivery-Notice" indicate this has happened.
	- Verbose-Logging=TRUE tells the BSMTP module to print all BSMTP
	  commands and responses on the console.

An additional attempt at preventing BSMTP mail loops is done by counting
the number of "Received:" headers and stopping the mail if it has
more than 50.

There are three new exits (see INSTALL LISTING for a description):
	- DISTLIST (BSMTP distribution lists)
	- PROFS (local PROFS delivery)
	- VMMLOG (Columbia VMM-format bulletin boards)

The SHOW command now has an alias of QUERY and an additional option of
ACTIVE (e.g. SHOW ACTIVE DOMAINS *).  Do not trust the statistics
collected though.  There are some places were statistics are not being
updated.

Source maintenance procedures now use the standard VM/SP EXECUPDT
utility for C source and H files.

